  "title": "Repairing Attachment Ruptures",
  "content": "Attachment ruptures happen in every family. A rupture is a moment of disconnection—yelling, shutting down, misunderstanding, or emotional withdrawal. Ruptures are not failures; they are opportunities. What matters most is repair. When parents repair after a rupture, children learn that relationships can recover and that emotions are safe.\n\nRepair involves acknowledging what happened, taking responsibility, and offering reassurance. It teaches children that mistakes do not break love. Parents who repair consistently raise children who are more resilient, more trusting, and more emotionally secure.\n\nThis lesson helps parents understand how to repair in a way that strengthens attachment rather than weakening it.",
  "activities": [
    "Think of a recent rupture with your child. Practice a repair statement such as 'I’m sorry I got frustrated. I care about you and I’m working on staying calm.'",
    "Notice how your child responds when you repair quickly versus when repair is delayed."
  ],
  "reflectionQuestions": [
    "How were ruptures handled in your childhood?",
    "What makes repair difficult for you?",
    "How does your child behave after a repair conversation?"
